# Rank 1112 / CVE-2026-40895: follow-redirects stripped only three hardcoded
# header names on a cross-domain or protocol-downgrade redirect, so any custom
# authentication header an application set, X-Api-Key, X-Auth-Token, and the
# like, followed the redirect to whatever host the response pointed at
# (CWE-200, CWE-212).
#
# RedirectableRequest.prototype._processResponse in index.js called
# removeMatchingHeaders(/^(?:(?:proxy-)?authorization|cookie)$/i, ...) on a
# cross-origin or downgraded redirect. Every header outside that fixed list,
# including any bearer token an application sent under its own header name,
# was copied to the next request unchanged.
#
# Pool commit 844c4d302ac9 is the real fix despite the COMMIT_NONE flag,
# checked against GHSA-r4q5-vmmm-2653 which it implements: it adds a
# `sensitiveHeaders` request option, builds `this._headerFilter` from the
# three built-in names plus whatever the caller supplies, and strips against
# that instead of the fixed regex. The three built-in names are unchanged, so
# a request that does not pass `sensitiveHeaders` behaves identically before
# and after the upgrade; only a caller that also adds its own header name to
# the option is protected. This is a different shape from the library-only
# bugs at ranks 1083, 1097, 1101, 1103 and 1105: there the application side
# was identical on both revisions and only the dependency mattered, but here
# the application has to change too, so the vulnerable and fixed forms differ
# in the request options an application passes, not only in which
# follow-redirects version is installed.
#
# ATTENTION, vocabulary gap. Scanning index.js at either revision reports the
# same one unrelated SSRF-shaped finding on the internal redirect URL
# construction; nothing labels the header-stripping call in either. The
# nearest concept by CWE, code.ExternalLinkReferrerLeak (CWE_200, an HTML
# template's external links leaking the current page's sensitive URL through
# the browser's own Referer header), shares the theme, a secret carried in an
# HTTP header crossing an origin boundary, but not the mechanism: this is a
# Referer leak from rendered markup, not a client library forwarding an
# application-named header across a redirect, and its only binding scans
# config/template files. No concept states "an HTTP client's redirect
# behavior forwards headers outside an explicit allowlist across an origin
# change." Stating it generically also needs knowing which of an
# application's own header names carry a secret, which nothing in the request
# options declares on its own.
#
# No definition change attempted.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-vocabulary-gap, both revisions rejected.

test "rank1112: a custom auth header has nothing telling the client to strip it on redirect"
  lang javascript
  file app.js
  reject VYQL-SEC-025
  code
  ```
  const followRedirects = require('follow-redirects');

  function fetchWithApiKey(url, apiKey) {
    return followRedirects.https.request(url, {
      headers: { 'X-Api-Key': apiKey },
    });
  }
  ```

test "rank1112: the custom header is named so the client strips it across origins"
  lang javascript
  file app.js
  reject VYQL-SEC-025
  code
  ```
  const followRedirects = require('follow-redirects');

  function fetchWithApiKey(url, apiKey) {
    return followRedirects.https.request(url, {
      headers: { 'X-Api-Key': apiKey },
      sensitiveHeaders: ['X-Api-Key'],
    });
  }
  ```
